Deliver this end-to-end: $ARGUMENTS

Run the pipeline in order, and don't advance a phase until the previous one is solid. Scale it to
the goal: for a small, clear goal, collapse phases 1–3 into a one-paragraph brief and get approval;
its criteria still name their Checks, a reported bug is still reproduced first, the project check is still named,
and the precedent sweep still runs before you present it.
{{ guidance }}

{% if inquire %}
## 0. Route the goal
Classify the goal before anything else, state the classification, and let me override it:
- **Build / change** — produce or modify something → continue with phases 1–6.
- **Understand** — learn how or why the code works, no change wanted yet → run the `/inquire` loop
  instead of this pipeline.
- **Mixed** — understand, then change → run a short `/inquire` orientation first, then continue with
  phase 1 using its ledger as context.
{% if brief %}- A brief from `/brief` (`.claude/briefs/<slug>.md`, with or without a leading `@`): Read it first, its
  `Target:` line is its route — `deliver` continues with phase 1; `inquire` is `/inquire`'s brief{% if workstreams %} and
  `workstreams` is `/workstreams`'{% endif %}: say so and stop.
  If no such file exists, say so (a brief written in a worktree stays in that worktree) and stop.
{% endif %}{% endif %}
## 1. Sharpen the goal
{% if intent %}If the goal names an intent from `/intent` — its intent file, an issue draft without one
(`.claude/intent/drafts/issue-*.md`), or an issue that links either (an intent file's `Issue:` line, a
draft's `<!-- Issue: … -->` line) — read it first and check its sign-off. An intent file needs
`Status: Accepted`, with every approver approved in its Sign-off section; an issue draft needs
`<!-- Status: Accepted -->`, with every approver ticked approved in its Needs from checklist{% if approvers %}
(this project's approvers: {{ approvers }}){% endif %}. If not, don't start — list who is still pending and
stop. If I insist, say plainly that starting now bypasses the approvers' sign-off, and start only after
I confirm explicitly. Then treat its approved plan as the brief: confirm with me what changed since,
and skip what it already settles in phases 1–3.
{% endif %}{% if brief %}If the goal is a brief from `/brief` (`.claude/briefs/<slug>.md`, with or without a leading `@`),
Read it first; its `Target:` line must name `deliver` (another target's brief belongs to that skill — say
so and stop). Treat it as my answers: its Goal, Scope, Success criteria (keep their slugs),
Constraints, Out of scope and Risks settle phases 1–2 and seed phase 3's task list, Scope line and
risk register; its Context is the skim already done (re-check a `file:line` that looks stale); its
Inferred items are mine unless I contest one. Ask only what its Open section leaves open, then
present the plan for approval as usual. `Status: draft` is usable — say it is unfinished. If no such
file exists, say so (a brief written in a worktree stays in that worktree) and stop.
{% endif %}{% if intent or brief %}An intent or a brief never settles these: each criterion's slug and Check, the project
check, the precedent sweep, and a reported bug's reproduction. Add what it lacks of them and present the plan for
approval before phase 4.
{% endif %}Rewrite the goal into a crisp brief using the prompt-engineering technique: explicit role, task,
and success criteria; remove ambiguity.

## 2. Gather requirements
First skim the relevant code (read-only, in parallel) so every question is informed — never ask
what the code already answers. Then interview me one question at a time — 2–4 default options
(recommended first), only what resolves real ambiguity; infer the rest and say what you inferred.
Cover scope, constraints, success criteria, and out-of-scope.

**Check the premise.** When the goal reports a bug or says something doesn't work, reproduce it before you
plan, even when a brief or an intent settles the rest of this phase: run the failing test, a command, or the
steps the report gives, and quote the output. Reproduce it here, locally: if that needs anything gated, destructive
or outside the project, ask me first. If it doesn't reproduce, say so with that output and ask me before
planning — never change code to fix what you haven't seen fail. When it does, trace it to its root cause: the code
that produces the wrong result, not the place where it shows. Search for every caller of each function whose
behavior will change; each one goes into the plan's tasks or its risk register.

**Find the project check.**
{% if check_cmd %}The project check is `{{ check_cmd }}`. Read what CI runs too (`.github/workflows/`, a Makefile, the
package's scripts) and add any check it runs that this one leaves out.{% else %}It is what CI runs (`.github/workflows/`,
a Makefile, the package's scripts): the tests, linters, formatters, and type and style checks, as commands you can
run here — together, the project check.{% endif %} A brief's Context may name it
already. The project check never changes a file: run each command in its checking form (`--check`, a dry run;
never `--fix` or `--write`). Name it in the plan; a check you can't run here is a risk.

## 3. Plan and get approval
Draft a numbered task list + intent + a **risk register** (every risk gets a mitigation task).
Give each success criterion a slug (the project rules' **Naming what you refer back to**) and a **Check**: the test
or command that proves it, and what it gives on the base now — it fails there, or doesn't exist yet. A criterion that
keeps existing behavior is marked `holds on base`: its Check passes there and must still pass at the end. A
criterion's Check belongs to the last task it needs; name that task. Order the work test-first: the task that meets
a criterion writes that criterion's test before the code that makes it pass.
{% if scope_guard %}Add a **Scope** line: what will change, what is out of scope, and any
refactor, rename or dependency change the goal needs. `{{ scope_guard }}` checks the change against the tasks, the
criteria and the Scope line in phase 5.
{% endif %}Before you present the plan, run the precedent sweep: {% if explorer %}the `{{ explorer }}` subagent{% else %}a built-in `Explore` subagent{% endif %} gets the draft
plan and lists the precedents the change must follow, by the project rule **Precedents**, and the callers that rely on
what will change. Fold them in — a precedent that changes a task changes the draft — and keep the list with the plan;
for a small goal keep the sweep short, but run it.
Present the plan with its precedent list and **invite push-back**; iterate until I approve. Do not execute yet.

## 4. Research in parallel (read-only)
Investigate concurrently where independent: fetch the relevant online HTML/PDF docs — from the
trusted documentation sites only (the project's **Web research** rule) — and study the local source. Run these in parallel — exploration is side-effect-free.
If approval changed the plan's tasks, run the precedent sweep again on what changed. If what you find — a precedent
included — contradicts the approved plan, go back to phase 3 and re-plan rather than pushing through — at most 2 re-plans;
after that, stop and present the conflict to me instead of looping.

## 5. Execute under the gates
**Baseline.** Before the first task, note the base{% if scope_guard %} for the scope check below{% endif %}: run
`git status --porcelain=v2 --branch --untracked-files=all` — its `# branch.oid` line is the commit you start
from, and the files it lists are mine, already modified. If you are on the base branch, cut a working branch first
and name it. If a file a task may change is among the files already modified, stop and ask me before the first task:
a worker's worktree starts from the last commit and can't see my edits. Then run the project check and every
criterion's Check that exists on the base, and keep their output. A Check passes only when it ran at least one test or
assertion for its criterion; one that selects nothing hasn't run. A check that fails here is a **pre-existing
failure**: leave it as it is — don't fix, skip, silence or delete it unless the plan says so — and report it apart
in phase 6. A criterion's Check that already passes on the base, unless the criterion is marked `holds on base`,
means the criterion is met already or the Check doesn't test it: stop and tell me.{% if check_cmd %} Except: if
`{{ check_cmd }}` fails on the base, every worker's stop gate fails too: stop and tell me before the first task.{% endif %}

Work the approved plan, one task at a time. Give {% if implementer_agent %}`{{ implementer_agent }}`{% else %}whoever makes the change{% endif %} one brief per task:
- the task as the plan words it, and the criteria it meets, each with its Check;
- the files it may change, and the precedents that bear on it, verbatim;
- the pre-existing failures, to leave alone;
- these rules: change only what the task needs, and list other improvements in your report instead of making them;
  write each criterion's test before the code; never change or delete an existing test or its assertions to make the
  change pass — stop and report the conflict, which I decide; if you have no shell, I run the Checks after you stop,
  so base your confidence on the code as you read it and name what only a run would show; end with the files you
  changed and a `Confidence: NN%` line.
{% if worktree_implementer %}
**Merging `{{ worktree_implementer }}`'s work.** `{{ worktree_implementer }}` works in its own git worktree, and its changes land on its
`worktree-agent-<id>` branch when it stops. Merge that branch into the working branch after each task and each rework,
before anything checks the change — or the next task starts without it — alone in its command:
`git merge --no-ff --no-edit worktree-agent-<id>`.{% if approval_gate %} The approval gate lets exactly that merge pass; any other merge,
a push or a deploy waits until I run `ocgen approve` in my own terminal, and a yes in chat doesn't open it.{% endif %}
If ocgen holds the merge to commit what the worker left uncommitted, run the same command once more. If git reports a
conflict, run `git merge --abort`, stop and tell me; if the merge is refused for any other reason, stop and tell me —
never get around it. If the loop guard released that worker, read its branch's diff
(`git diff HEAD...worktree-agent-<id>`) and tell me what it holds before you merge it.
{% endif %}
After each task and each rework, run the project check and that task's Checks yourself — whoever makes the change may
have no shell — and compare with the baseline: a new failure, or a Check that still fails, goes back with its output
as that task's next attempt. For each decision or completed task, **state a `Confidence: NN%`** and
surface gated decisions for me to confirm; keep working a risk until its mitigation round is done.
Give each task at most 3 attempts: if the same approach fails twice, change it; on the third
failure, stop and report the blocker with evidence.
Do not perform high-impact external actions (deploys, `git push`, {% if worktree_implementer %}any merge but the one of its branch above{% else %}a merge{% endif %},
cloud mutations) without my explicit approval{% if approval_gate %} — `ocgen approve`, from my own terminal{% endif %}.
{% if scope_guard %}
**Scope check.** When the plan's tasks are done{% if adversary %}, and before the adversary check{% endif %},
have `{{ scope_guard }}` check the whole change against the plan in both directions — nothing planned missing, nothing
unplanned added — the scope loop in `.claude/rules/ocgen-team.md`:
{% if implementer_isolated %}- First merge `{{ implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch, or it checks code without the change.
{% endif %}- Give it the goal and the approved plan verbatim (after a re-plan, the newest one; if phases 1–3 collapsed, the brief;
  if an approved intent replaced them, its plan and acceptance criteria), the decisions I confirmed, the base,
  and the files that were already modified.
- Cuts: revert each one yourself, exactly as it says, and write nothing new; then run the project check, and
  undo any revert that breaks it.
- `Verdict: INCOMPLETE`: send {% if implementer %}`{{ implementer }}`{% else %}the subagent that made the change{% endif %} only the Partial and Missing items, verbatim, then have
  `{{ scope_guard }}` check the whole change once more. At most {{ scope_rounds }} completion round: an item still
  missing after it is **not done** in phase 6, never done.
- Differs (built another way than planned): don't rework it; show it to me in phase 6.
{% else %}
**Final check.** When the plan's tasks are done{% if adversary %}, and before the adversary check{% endif %}, check the
change against every success criterion in the approved plan (after a re-plan, the newest one; if phases 1–3
collapsed, the brief; if an approved intent replaced them, its acceptance criteria), and run the tests{% if worktree_implementer %} —
after merging `{{ worktree_implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch, or you check code without the change{% endif %}. For a
criterion that isn't met or a test that fails, go back to the task that should meet it, fix it, and check again —
at most 2 rounds. A criterion still unmet after that is **not done** in phase 6, never done.
{% endif %}
{% if reviewer %}**Review check.** After the {% if scope_guard %}scope{% else %}final{% endif %} check{% if adversary %}, and before the adversary check{% endif %}, have three independent `{{ reviewer }}` runs
check the whole change at once, one per angle — `conformance`, `correctness`, `tests` —
and fresh `{{ reviewer }}` runs confirm the findings: the review loop in `.claude/rules/ocgen-team.md`.
{% if worktree_implementer %}- Merge `{{ worktree_implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch before each wave, or it checks code without the change.
